          ],
          "content": "**Figure it Out**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.194,
            0.882,
            0.231
          ],
          "content": "**Q.4. Get a slanting crease on the paper. Now, try to get another crease that is perpendicular to the slanting crease.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.239,
            0.882,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": "**a. How many right angles do you have now? Justify why the angles are exact right angles.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.284,
            0.881,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": "**b. Describe how you folded the paper so that any other person who doesn’t know the process can simply follow your description to get the right angle.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.329,
            0.672,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": "Ans. a. Four right angle. Each angle is ¼ the of the complete angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.356,
            0.47,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": "b. Explore different ways of doing it."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.401,
            0.19,
            0.419
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Page 31"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.428,
            0.239,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "**Figure it Out**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.455,
            0.881,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": "**Q.2. Make a few acute angles and a few obtuse angles. Draw them in different orientations.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.501,
            0.282,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "Ans. Acute angle"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.312,
            0.49,
            0.64,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_47_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.582,
            0.29,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "Obtuse angle"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.564,
            0.64,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_47_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.637,
            0.882,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": "**Q.3. Do you know what the words acute and obtuse mean? Acute means sharp and obtuse means blunt. Why do you think these words have been chosen?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.681,
            0.882,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "Ans. In acute angles the opening of the edges is lesser than the obtuse angle which have larger openings."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.726,
            0.883,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": "**Q.4. Find out the number of acute angles in each of the figures below. What will be the next figure and how many acute angles will it have? Do you notice any pattern in the numbers?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.789,
            0.251,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": "Ans. (i) three"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.816,
            0.276,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Twelve"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.835,
            0.318,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Twenty-one"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.852,
            0.545,
            0.868
          ],
          "content": "The next figure will have thirty acute angles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.869,
            0.68,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": "Yes, the pattern is $3 \\times 0 + 1 , 3 \\times 1 + 1 , 3 \\times 2 + 1 , 3 \\times 3 + 1 , \\ldots$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.888,
            0.641,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "The number 0,1,2,3,4,….. are number of inner triangles."
        },
